# Line endings.
#
# The repo has always stored these LF (`git ls-files --eol` shows `i/lf`), but with
# `core.autocrlf=true` and no attributes file the WORKING TREE got whatever the checkout
# felt like — which is how `scripts/ue5_freeze_helper.lua` ended up CRLF while
# `scripts/ue5_invoke_helper.lua` stayed LF on the same machine. That divergence is what
# produced `[FREEZEINJECT-CRLF-2026-08-20]`: the freeze helper's 1,137 CRLF endings made
# its byte count disagree with the LF-normalised copy Cheat Engine stores, and the
# post-write check reported a good write as a failure.
#
# Pinning `eol=lf` keeps the checkout matching the index so the two can't drift apart
# again. The code no longer DEPENDS on it — CeLuaHygiene.NormalizeTableFilePayload folds
# the payload before it is handed to CE either way — but a tree that agrees with itself
# is one less thing that behaves differently on a fresh clone.
*.lua text eol=lf

# Lua rigs and the CE table are read by the same tooling.
*.CT   text eol=lf

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EVERYTHING ELSE — added 2026-08-23 after a staged-build session kept having to
# work around this.
#
# THE PROBLEM IS THAT `core.autocrlf` IS MACHINE-LOCAL. Measured on this PC:
# it is unset at --local and --global and `true` at --system, i.e. the
# Git-for-Windows installer default living in C:\Program Files\Git\etc\gitconfig.
# That file is not in the repo and does not travel, so the two development PCs can
# check the same commit out with different line endings. `.gitattributes` DOES
# travel, which is why the rule belongs here rather than in a git config.
#
# The index is already uniform: 1,021 of 1,035 tracked files are `i/lf`. The WORKING
# TREE was not — 339 `w/lf`, 679 `w/crlf`, 3 `w/mixed`. Pinning eol=lf makes the
# checkout match the index everywhere.
#
# ⚠ WHY IT MATTERED, concretely: a file that is `i/lf w/lf` gets REWRITTEN TO CRLF by
# `git checkout`, and `git status` stays CLEAN afterwards because the index is
# unchanged. So `git checkout -- <file>` could not be trusted to revert a staged
# experiment byte-for-byte — every staged row in this session reverted from a byte
# snapshot instead. With eol=lf a checkout is byte-safe again.
#
# `text=auto`, never a bare `text`: it lets git keep auto-detecting binaries, so
# `dll/src/VersionNeedleScan.h` (a deliberate NUL inside a comment about
# NUL-terminated needles) and the image/gz assets are left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ⛔ WINDOWS BATCH MUST BE CRLF IN THE WORKING TREE -- the one exception to the line above.
#
# cmd.exe resolves `goto :label` by SEEKING to a byte offset and re-reading from there. Strip the
# CRs and every offset shifts, so the label is searched for in the wrong place and the script dies
# with a message that names the label but not the cause:
#
#     The system cannot find the batch label specified - parse_args
#
# ⭐ REPRODUCED 2026-09-06, and it had already bitten the second PC. `build.cmd` (6 labels, 15
# gotos) fails on a FRESH CHECKOUT, which is what `eol=lf` produces; the same file with CRLF parses
# its arguments correctly. It worked on the first machine only because that working tree still held
# `w/crlf` from before `eol=lf` was pinned and was never re-checked-out -- `git ls-files --eol`
# showed `i/lf w/crlf` there while every other text file showed `w/lf`. A bug that only appears on
# a clean clone is the worst kind to leave to chance.
#
# ⚠ The EXTENSION is pinned, not the one file that fails today. `count_cloc.bat` also uses a label
# and happens to survive, because whether the shifted seek lands somewhere fatal depends on where
# the label sits -- "happens to survive" is not a property to build on, and a `.cmd` that gains a
# label later would break silently on someone else's clone.
#
# ⚠ PowerShell is deliberately NOT included. It has a real parser, not a byte-seeking one:
# `build.ps1` is `w/lf` here and runs fine, clean AOT publish included. `.ps1`, `.sh` and
# everything else stay LF.
#
# This changes the CHECKOUT only; the index stays LF, which is what keeps the repo portable.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Preserved log evidence — VERBATIM ARTIFACTS, so never normalise them.
#
# `docs/evidence/` holds runtime logs that a committed claim rests on, copied out of
# `%LOCALAPPDATA%\UE5CEDumper\Logs\` before the 21-day age sweep
# (`Grimoire::LOG_RETENTION_DAYS`) deletes them. The point of the copy is that the bytes are
# what the DLL actually wrote, so `eol=lf` must NOT rewrite their CRLF endings — a
# re-formatted artifact is a quotation, not evidence. `-text` disables both the checkout
# conversion and the index normalisation, while leaving them diffable (unlike `binary`,
# which would also suppress the diff a reviewer needs).
docs/evidence/** -text

# Binary assets — never transform, never diff as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.gz   binary
